  "summary": "Distinctive architectural style characterized by organic forms, mud-brick construction, and vertical elements in Sahelian regions.",
  "description": "Features include banco (mud) walls with projecting wooden beams for scaffolding, conical minarets, and inward-focused courtyards. Developed as an adaptation to the hot, arid climate with limited building materials, creating thermally efficient structures.",
  "culturalContext": "Sudano-Sahelian building grew from the Sahel corridor between desert and savanna, where West African mason families — notably the Mande guilds credited with Djenné and Timbuktu — fused local banco technique with the demands of trans-Saharan trade towns and Islamic worship. Mali's Djenné and Timbuktu, Niger's Agadez and northern Nigeria's cities kept the style alive; the 1907 Djenné mosque rebuild fixed its canonical look, and annual communal replastering keeps the tradition a living social institution.",
